WEBVTT

00:00.000 --> 00:02.080
What does a breach actually cost you?

00:02.080 --> 00:04.800
Not a red, amber, green dot on a dashboard,

00:04.800 --> 00:06.480
a number in pounds.

00:06.480 --> 00:10.240
Most governance ignores that, compliant or not,

00:10.240 --> 00:12.740
a green tick that price nothing and move nothing,

00:12.740 --> 00:13.920
a binary lie.

00:13.920 --> 00:16.700
But here's the idea, governance is not a checkbox.

00:16.700 --> 00:20.080
It is a proportionate, continuously retuned response

00:20.080 --> 00:23.020
to quantified risk, and it is all one thing,

00:23.020 --> 00:26.480
the policy, how your organization actually operates,

00:26.480 --> 00:27.920
written down as code.

00:27.920 --> 00:30.200
Start where your engineers already live.

00:30.200 --> 00:32.840
Policy is just a dependency, a lint pack.

00:32.840 --> 00:35.900
You pin it, you sign it, you adopt it by pull request.

00:35.900 --> 00:38.440
Governance becomes something a developer already knows

00:38.440 --> 00:39.840
how to consume and trust.

00:39.840 --> 00:43.220
Now, the moment that makes it land, two institutions,

00:43.220 --> 00:45.400
a retailer and a hospital.

00:45.400 --> 00:49.000
Same platform, same engine, one identical rule,

00:49.000 --> 00:50.760
encrypt data at rest.

00:50.760 --> 00:52.880
Watch what proportionality actually does

00:52.880 --> 00:54.240
to that single control.

00:54.240 --> 00:56.040
In the retailer, it merely audits.

00:56.040 --> 00:58.480
In the hospital, the very same rule blocks.

00:58.480 --> 00:59.320
Why?

00:59.320 --> 01:01.580
Because a health breach costs far more,

01:01.580 --> 01:03.720
so the model prices it far higher.

01:03.720 --> 01:07.720
Same control, opposite verdict, because the pounds say so.

01:07.720 --> 01:10.960
But real governance is never just yes or no.

01:10.960 --> 01:14.320
Fall behind on patching and your workload still runs

01:14.320 --> 01:18.160
but caged, throttled, sandboxed, watched more closely,

01:18.160 --> 01:21.060
least trusted, most expensive to run.

01:21.060 --> 01:23.780
A spectrum of consequence, not a cliff edge,

01:23.820 --> 01:28.140
and that cage costs money while still leaving risk behind.

01:28.140 --> 01:32.100
So staying current becomes provably the cheaper path.

01:32.100 --> 01:34.100
Add it up, the risk you keep

01:34.100 --> 01:36.100
plus the cost of every control.

01:36.100 --> 01:38.860
That is your true total cost of risk.

01:38.860 --> 01:41.180
These pounds are real, not guessed.

01:41.180 --> 01:44.540
Calibrated estimates, a Monte Carlo simulation,

01:44.540 --> 01:46.900
and the tail your board actually fears

01:46.900 --> 01:49.260
the insurance industry's own maths.

01:49.260 --> 01:51.600
Tighten the control, the number falls.

01:51.600 --> 01:53.740
Accept a risk, it rises.

01:53.740 --> 01:55.580
Live on screen.

01:55.580 --> 01:58.580
So we kill the ugliest word in governance.

01:58.580 --> 02:01.620
Exemption, no carve outs, no favors.

02:01.620 --> 02:04.940
Instead, you may do this if you meet these conditions.

02:04.940 --> 02:07.300
Uniform version priced.

02:07.300 --> 02:09.840
Every allowance carries its own honest line

02:09.840 --> 02:11.020
of accepted risk.

02:11.020 --> 02:12.120
Here's the leap.

02:12.120 --> 02:15.260
The policy version you satisfy becomes your identity.

02:15.260 --> 02:17.460
Every workload, every person,

02:17.460 --> 02:19.960
every device carries it cryptographically.

02:19.960 --> 02:22.580
A sensitive service simply refuses anyone

02:22.580 --> 02:23.940
who has fallen out of date.

02:23.940 --> 02:26.100
Zero trust, finally made real.

02:26.100 --> 02:27.180
Failures move left.

02:27.180 --> 02:30.580
The cluster advertises which policy versions it accepts.

02:30.580 --> 02:32.380
Your pipeline runs the exact check

02:32.380 --> 02:34.860
the cluster will offline before you merge.

02:34.860 --> 02:37.680
A deploy time surprise becomes practically unheard of.

02:37.680 --> 02:40.020
The compliant path becomes the easy path.

02:40.020 --> 02:43.340
Then it comes alive, the estate war games itself.

02:43.340 --> 02:46.020
Threat feeds, new vulnerabilities,

02:46.020 --> 02:48.580
regulator fines, all pour in.

02:48.580 --> 02:50.460
An agent stress tests your controls

02:50.460 --> 02:53.720
and asks one question, is this still proportionate

02:53.720 --> 02:55.500
or has the world quietly moved?

02:55.500 --> 02:58.560
When it drifts, the agent does exactly one thing.

02:58.560 --> 02:59.980
It opens a pull request.

02:59.980 --> 03:02.900
It proposes, it never disposes.

03:02.900 --> 03:05.380
A human reviews, the gate checks,

03:05.380 --> 03:07.220
and only then does it ship.

03:07.220 --> 03:10.340
A powerful machine safe on your existing rails.

03:10.340 --> 03:14.420
And every actor, human or machine, signs their work.

03:14.420 --> 03:17.020
Cryptographically signed, publicly logged,

03:17.020 --> 03:18.980
verifiable end to end.

03:18.980 --> 03:21.700
You can prove which actor changed what, when,

03:21.700 --> 03:24.940
and from what evidence for an AI-enabled business.

03:24.940 --> 03:26.260
That is how you trust it.

03:26.260 --> 03:28.500
So what does your funding build?

03:28.500 --> 03:33.020
Six real organizations brought to life a shared platform.

03:33.020 --> 03:36.380
Three institutions, two regulators publishing controls

03:36.380 --> 03:37.620
and fines as code.

03:37.620 --> 03:39.860
A cluster each where every core claim

03:39.860 --> 03:41.820
is demonstrated, not slideware.

03:41.820 --> 03:43.540
And here's why this is ours to build.

03:43.540 --> 03:45.420
Flux is not a logo on a slide.

03:45.460 --> 03:48.420
It is load-bearing, doing six real jobs,

03:48.420 --> 03:51.980
distribution, provenance, pruning, healing, ordering,

03:51.980 --> 03:53.100
alerts.

03:53.100 --> 03:56.300
This puts Flux at the very center of the risk story,

03:56.300 --> 03:58.380
which lets us close where no security

03:58.380 --> 04:00.980
talk ever closes, the balance sheet.

04:00.980 --> 04:03.660
Residual risk becomes economic capital.

04:03.660 --> 04:06.880
The very same controls an underwriter would price.

04:06.880 --> 04:10.180
Technological risk, finally, a line your board can read,

04:10.180 --> 04:11.540
defend and act on.

04:11.540 --> 04:14.620
For the spend, you get three things that compound.

04:14.620 --> 04:17.180
A flagship talk that tours every conference,

04:17.180 --> 04:20.420
a demo estate our teams put in front of any prospect,

04:20.420 --> 04:23.940
and ownership of a story no competitor in this market

04:23.940 --> 04:25.100
is telling yet.

04:25.100 --> 04:26.980
And the timing is the whole point.

04:26.980 --> 04:29.860
Every board is asking how to trust AI

04:29.860 --> 04:31.120
inside their business.

04:31.120 --> 04:34.500
We answer it provably, on stage, in pounds.

04:34.500 --> 04:36.860
Whoever tells this story first owns it,

04:36.860 --> 04:38.780
and that window is open right now.

04:38.780 --> 04:39.940
So here's the ask.

04:39.940 --> 04:42.060
Fund the full build every step

04:42.060 --> 04:43.640
through to the flagship talk.

04:43.680 --> 04:44.900
Give me that runway.

04:44.900 --> 04:46.760
And I'll hand you the estate that proves governance

04:46.760 --> 04:49.680
can finally be proportionate, honest, and alive.

04:49.680 --> 04:51.520
Let's put risk on the balance sheet.

